 subclass raised by the helper and caught at the entry point, printing one line
 that names the value and the class.
 
+## One line: the grammar, all eight allocating modes (round 55)
+
+The class is one operator-visible line on stderr and exit 2. A caller parses the
+line; it is not a shared Python type — each of the seven tools defines its own
+`ScratchRootUnusable(RuntimeError)` beside its own copy of
+`strict_scratch_root()`, and no module imports a common class. That is why the
+claim is the grammar below and not "the tools raise one type": two of the seven
+raised a bare `RuntimeError`, and one printed the failure on stdout
+(`SELFTEST: FAIL`), until round 55 moved both to this line.
+
+```
+^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*(\.py)?: could-not-evaluate: cannot create clone scratch under the scratch root: TMPDIR is not a usable directory: '<value>' \(could-not-evaluate\)$
+```
+
+The prefix is the tool's own name (`repeat-control.py`, `context-capture.py`,
+`pin-disposition.py`, `evidence-disposition.py`, `registry-check`,
+`ENVDIFF_SETUP`), so a caller may match the whole line or the suffix from
+`: could-not-evaluate:`. The value is the raw `TMPDIR` in single quotes; five
+helpers render it with `%r` and the two round-55 fixes with an explicit `'%s'`,
+so a `TMPDIR` containing a single quote would leave the five outside the grammar —
+observed by construction, and outside the counter's fixtures.
+
+### The eight allocating modes
+
+Each of these allocates clone scratch and must exit 2 with that line on stderr
+and nothing on stdout when `TMPDIR` is set but unusable (round-55 C2 arm
+`grammar`):
+
+| mode | tool |
+|---|---|
+| `repeat-control --selftest` | tools/repeat-control.py |
+| `registry-check --selftest` | tools/registry-check.py |
+| `bash-witness-study selftest` | tools/bash-witness-study.py (the round-55 counter's label for this row is `bash-witness-study --selftest`; the invocation takes a subcommand, not a flag) |
+| `pin-disposition --selftest` | tools/pin-disposition.py |
+| `evidence-disposition --selftest` | tools/evidence-disposition.py |
+| `context-capture --selftest` | tools/context-capture.py |
+| `env-diff --selftest` | tools/env-diff.py |
+| `env-diff --contract` | tools/env-diff.py |
+
+The last one is the mode round 54 left outside the contract: it is not a
+self-test, and it allocates its scratch only after the contract file parses.
+
+### The five lazy modes
+
+These do not touch the scratch root, so the same unusable `TMPDIR` is not an
+environment fact for them and they keep their ordinary exit (round-55 C2 arm
+`lazy`):
+
+| mode | expected exit on unusable `TMPDIR` |
+|---|---|
+| `repeat-control --snapshot` | 0 (fixture matches) |
+| `bash-witness-study count` | 0 |
+| `pin-disposition --read` | 0 |
+| `evidence-disposition --json` | 0 |
+| `context-capture --check` | 0 |
+
 ## What measures it
 
-`python3 .ufo/evidence/round-54/c2/measure.py` runs each of the seven tools'
-self-tests under three `TMPDIR` states (unusable, empty, usable) and reads the
-registry rows: 7/7 exit 2 with the naming line and no traceback, 7/7 exit 0 on
-the empty arm, 7/7 exit 0 on a usable root with no `mkdir` under `/tmp` under
-`strace`, and 7/7 rows carrying the `scratch_root` cell. The counter's blind
-spot is stated in the contract: it sees the self-test path, not every branch a
-tool might use to allocate scratch.
+`python3 .ufo/evidence/round-55/c2/measure.py` widens that to every documented
+mode of the seven tools — 13 modes under three `TMPDIR` states: the 8
+allocating modes exit 2 with the line above and no `RuntimeError`/`Traceback` on
+either stream, the 5 lazy modes keep their exit, all 13 keep their expected exit
+on the empty and the usable arms, the allocating modes make no `mkdir` under
+`/tmp` under `strace`, and the seven registry cells, this note and `SKILL.md`
+carry the grammar (round 54's counter saw only the self-tests). The remaining
+blind spot stands: a mode the registry does not document may allocate scratch on
+its first statement, and the counter checks that the line is present, not how a
+caller parses it — by prefix or as the whole line.
 
 ## Falsifier
 
 A tool that returns 0 after printing the could-not-evaluate line (control
 CTL_EXIT0), or that names the class without the value, fails the counter.
 
